425:  [\iota_\gamma\omega]=[\delta],\quad
426:  [\iota_\gamma\eta_3]=\eta_2,\quad
427:  [\iota_\gamma\eta_4]=[uw\delta].
428: \]
429: 
430: For an arbitrary integral affine origin correction, the same
431: calculation is exactly its \(\psi\)-coordinate; source and line
432: circle coordinates contribute zero to all four projections.
433: Thus every displayed differential is multiplication by one for
434: the actual glued family.  There are no higher differentials,
435: because the base has dimension two.  Only
436: \(H^0=H^6=\mathbf Z\) remains, without torsion or extension
437: ambiguity.  Together with simple connectivity this proves that
438: the total manifold is a homotopy six-sphere, and \(\Theta_6=0\)
439: identifies its smooth oriented type with the standard sphere.
440: 
441: **Proof: the closed positive parameter loop.** Write \(t_c\) for
442: the chosen nodal zero and put
443: \(h(t)=(t-t_c)/t\).  Since
444: \(\gamma(z,v+1)=\gamma(z,v)+\delta\), the smooth logarithmic
445: gluing changes by \((\log t)/(2\pi i)\delta\) under the
446: positive parameter deck.  The global ordinary deck is
447: \(-\log h(t)\delta/(2\pi i)\): near \(t=0\) it has exactly
448: that value up to a holomorphic unit logarithm, which extends on
449: the cap.  At the cusp use \(q=h(t)\), \(s=\log q/(2\pi i)\).
450: Then the actual periods are
451: 
452: \[
453:  \gamma(s,v)=\binom{\Gamma(q)}{C(q)+s+v},\qquad
454:  \delta=\binom01,
455: \]
456: 
457: with \(\Gamma,C\) holomorphic at zero, and the deck translates
458: by \(-s\delta\).  Its line-character multiplier \(q^{-1}\)
459: extends through the cusp: compose an inverse gamma lattice shift
460: with the bounded source translation \(\Gamma\) and the
461: nonvanishing multiplier \(\lambda e^{2\pi iC}\).  At every
462: other original fibre \(h\) is a unit.  This proves that the
463: glued family descends holomorphically over the punctured
464: \(\lambda\)-disc, with the stated positive closing map.
465: 
466: For a completely marked angular comparison use the auxiliary
467: logarithmic cover \(q=Q_0^2\), and the two origins
468: 
469: \[
470:  o=\binom{-\Gamma s}{-Cs-s^2/2-sv},\qquad
471:  \sigma=\binom{\Gamma v}{Cv+(v^2-v)/2}.
472: \]
473: 
474: The first has parameter difference \(-s\delta\); the second
475: has difference \(-s\delta+\gamma\).  Under \(s\mapsto s+2\),
476: their differences are \(-2\gamma-2\delta\) and zero.  Thus
477: they descend on this auxiliary cover.  In contrast, the ordinary
478: origin has one-step difference \(-\gamma-\delta/2\); we do
479: not impose a false one-step periodicity.  The cusp origin extends
480: by its bounded unit characters.  Subtracting the ordinary origin
481: removes the parameter deck and gives the one-node parameter chain
482: \(X_i=V/\lambda^i\), \(Y_i=\lambda^{i+1}/V\),
483: \(X_iY_i=\lambda\), over every nonzero \(q\).  Its origin
484: extends.  These are the two extending collar origins.  No proper
485: two-variable corner or descent of such a corner is used.
486: 
487: Their exact difference is
488: 
489: \[
490:  F=\binom{\Gamma(s+v)}{C(s+v)+(s+v)^2/2-v/2},
491: \]
492: 
493: and hence
494: 
495: \[
496:  F(s,v+\theta)-F(s,v)
497:  =\theta\gamma(s,v)+\frac{\theta(\theta-1)}2\delta.
498: \]
499: 
500: The additional line gauge is periodic, equals one at both
501: endpoints, and vanishes in the circle quotient.  The double cusp
502: deck changes this angular difference by \(2\theta\delta\),
503: so the retained line gauge has winding two.  In particular the
504: actual positive \(\lambda\)-loop makes one positive gamma turn,
505: with no parameter cover.  Every identity is executed in
506: `code/part3_fourfold/os45_origins.py`.
507: 
508: **Proof: the actual page and the return index.** The line circle is
509: free away from the selected rank-two cusp.  Indeed its equality
510: with an iterate of the contracting lift forces that iterate to
511: be zero by the norm inequality, and then forces the circle
512: element to be the identity.  This includes the whole \(I_8\)
513: neighbourhood, where translation permutes the components.  The
514: ordinary logarithmic cap is free as well.  At the single cusp
